<rdf:Description rdf:about=""  xmlns:pdf="http://ns.adobe.com/pdf/1.3/">
<pdf:Producer>MicrosoftÂ® Word for Microsoft 365</pdf:Producer><pdf:Keywords>Land owner appeals the dismissal of its counterclaim in a "regulatory taking" after being enjoined from using its property for commercial purposes.  A claim seeking compensation under 42 U.S.C. Â§ 1983 for a regulatory taking of property is subject to the four-year statute of limitations period in R.C. 2305.09(E), rather than the two-year period in R.C. 2305.10. Section 1983 is applicable to Ohio state actors and Ohio courts have concurrent jurisdiction with federal courts to entertain Section 1983 claims. A party filing a 1983 regulatory "taking" action in Ohio courts is not prohibited by the party's failure to first file a mandamus action under Ohio law to trigger appropriation proceedings. Ohio law does not preclude the filing of a declaratory judgment action for pursuing an as-applied constitutional challenge to a zoning regulation. Property owner's application for, and denial of, a variance application for commercial use by the board of zoning appeals demonstrates a "final decision" that is ripe for constitutional review and does not require the property owner to exhaust its administrative remedies prior to filing a declaratory judgment action challenging the constitutionality of a zoning regulation. </pdf:Keywords></rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about=""  xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">
<dc:title><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Dickard v. Jackson & Jackson, LLC</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:title><dc:creator><rdf:Seq><rdf:li>DONOVAN</rdf:li></rdf:Seq></dc:creator><dc:description><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">CA2025-07-072</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:description></rdf:Description>
